When hallmark legislation was signed into law a year ago, Virginia Marine Resources Commission officials smiled with relief. Finally they had the tools to manage the Chesapeake Bay wisely. Nobody is smiling now. The tools for wise men have become crutches to keep the past hobbling along.
In its simplest explanation, the Chesapeake Bay suffers because too many people catch too many fish. Habitat degradation, such as the loss of aquatic grass beds, has drastically reduced the number of places bay fish can spawn or use as a nursery. Water tainted by municipal and industrial discharge and chemical runoff from farms and city lawns has made it tougher for fish to survive. Nature itself plays a role.
Most fish reproduce in cycles - some years reproduction is poor and other years it is successful.
