Yet there exists a surplus of viable candidates, each with impressive experience and credentials, ready to step in at the first offering. How could you possibly go wrong choosing between so many qualified candidates? It��s a double-edged sword for employers: the number of qualified candidates does provide a larger talent pool … but that does not guarantee that they will catch the right fish.
You��re swimming in that talent pool now, trying to make yourself stand out. Here are some innovative ideas to do so:
1. Agree to work on contract: Often, employers feel forced to act against employees that are not doing even the bare minimum. Many companies use severance agreements to mitigate the ease of separating employees, but even these do require their own bit of legwork. Agreeing to work on contract frees the employer from a severance agreement, and also allows for a review of your performance to date. Before the contract ends, you should have been able to prove your worth to the organization. Eli Lilly and Company is one company that has started to hire representatives on contract. It enables them to adjust the size their sales force with much less logistics or financial strain.
2. Suggest working on a trial basis: This may seem too far out of the box, but imagine it through a different set of glasses: Would you buy a car before a test drive? Of course not. In certain situations, you may be able to show your prospective boss that you have the chops to start making an impact right away. Offer to do it for free for a short time – and if the hiring manager likes what they see, they can offer you the job. It also allows the employer if there is good chemistry between you, the manager, and the rest of the team. An added benefit is that this can give you additional leverage when negotiating your salary.
3. Demonstrate past success: One way of making your resume work for you is to bring supporting documentation. Make a copy of any files, awards, or other records that support the accomplishments highlighted in your resume. Give the employer the copy, and they will have something to remember you by long after the interview has ceased. It will let the interviewer review your documentation and qualifications at their leisure without the time constraints of the interview.
