That��s what needs to happen in the state Capitol if Texas lawmakers want to avoid another missed opportunity to raise money for the anemic highway fund.
Let the egos fall by the wayside. Lawmakers must pick between competing House and Senate constitutional amendments that would begin to bridge the $4 billion-a-year shortfall for road-funding needs. Neither would solve the problem, but either would be a start.
The most sellable option is probably the Senate��s version, SJR 1, which passed the upper chamber unanimously. It��s the simplest and could be explained in an elevator ride. With voter approval, it would take the taxes on oil and gas production that now flow into the rainy day fund and send half to the highway fund. It would also guarantee a minimum balance for the rainy day fund if oil production falls off.
Another thing going for SJR 1 is that the House passed a version of it last month, in the first special session called by Gov. Rick Perry. The House knows the ins and outs of this and mustered bipartisan support, even from tea party Republicans.
