First, it expressed concern about the difficulty military spouses face in obtaining and sustaining gainful employment, especially in small communities. Daigle recommended that National Defence and the Canadian Forces develop a national employment strategy to assist spouses of Forces personnel.
Accessing and maintaining health care during mandatory moves is also a persistent challenge for military families, according to the report. Most Canadian Forces families receive no military medical care and rely on provincial health care services. Military families often find it difficult to secure a family doctor or appointments with medical specialists but, unlike most Canadians, face frequent relocation that complicates those efforts.
