Gov. Dannel P. Malloy's plan to prohibit people on government watch lists from buying guns in the state makes sense on its face, but will likely bring a court challenge on due process if it goes forward. Can you deny someone's constitutional right to own a gun if the person is only suspected — not convicted — of being a terrorist?
Mr. Malloy said he plans to ask federal authorities for access to their databases, including the no-fly roster. If and when that is granted, Mr. Malloy said he will sign an order requiring those who apply for gun permits to be screened against the government lists — essentially an expansion of the state's current background check process. Those who are on such lists would be banned from purchasing guns and ammunition. In addition, anyone with an existing permit who is found to be on the lists would have the permit revoked.
He added there would be an appeals process for people who say they have been unfairly placed on government watch lists. There already is such a process, as it happens, but it is secret and so there is no way of telling how well it works.
Database
The watch lists in question come from the FBI's Terrorist Screening Database, sometimes called the terrorist watch list, the government's "consolidated database containing the identity information of known or suspected terrorists." The no-fly list is a subset of the database. There is no problem keeping known terrorists off planes or out of gun stores. The larger civil liberties issue is with those merely suspected of planning or carrying out terrorist activities. Usually we don't penalize people unless they are convicted of a crime.
The FBI says there are multilevel reviews before someone is placed on the list, and continual updates to it. But the American Civil Liberties Union, which is suing the government over the no-fly list, told the Los Angeles Times that the watch and no-fly lists include thousands of names that have been added in error, as well as the names of family members of suspected terrorists.
It is hard to know who is right because the lists are kept secret. Indeed, people are not notified when they are put on the list, nor why, and usually don't find out until they try to travel. Also, the ACLU estimates that there are more than 1.1 million names in the database, and that the vast majority are noncitizens living oversees, who would generally not be eligible to buy a gun in the U.S. in the first place.
